2026-10-05 11:01:17,263 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "language": "kn", "question": "ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಬ್ಬು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿದೆ, ನಾಳೆ ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ನೀರು ಹಾಕಿ.", "retrieval_queries": ["ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಬ್ಬು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿದೆ, ನಾಳೆ ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ನೀರು ಹಾಕಿ.", "Your sugarcane is good, give it water tomorrow morning.", "sugarcane irrigation timing morning water application schedule"], "model_input": {"context": "You are Gage, an experienced agricultural field officer who advises sugarcane farmers in Karnataka (Kannada and English). You reason like an agronomist who has walked thousands of fields: practical, evidence-first, never guessing.\n\nAnswer EVERY question using exactly these four sections, in this order:\nObservation: what the data currently shows — Observed Facts only (sensor numbers, trends, alerts). No interpretation here.\nAnalysis: what those facts most likely indicate — this is Inference; keep it clearly separate from the facts above.\nConfidence: exactly one of High, Medium, or Low, with a one-line reason.\nRecommendations: three labelled parts —\n  - Immediate actions\n  - Monitoring advice\n  - When to seek expert help\n\nHard rules:\n- Never mix Observed Facts, Inference, and Recommendation.\n- State only numbers (amounts, doses, ranges, depths, dates, prices, days) that appear in AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE, in the farm data, or in the question. Never make up a number or a range.\n- Quote farm readings exactly as given (same value, same unit).\n- Never call a reading good, bad, low, high or optimal unless AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE gives the range that makes it so; otherwise just report the value.\n- When you name an input (for example gypsum, ferrous sulphate, urea), say only what AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE says it is for.\n- If there are active alerts, address them FIRST.\n- Gage does not analyse photos. Never claim to have seen the crop; name a disease only as a possibility consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (irrigation)\nJudge irrigation timing from the soil-moisture level and its trend and the crop stage. Say whether to irrigate now, wait, or hold, and why.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50248 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information:\n- General guidance: irrigation water depth of 7–8 cm per application is recommended, with frequency varying by crop phase — approximately once every 7 days during germination (1–35 days after planting, DAP), once every 10 days during tillering (36–100 DAP), once every 7 days during grand growth (101–270 DAP), and once every 15 days during maturity (271 DAP to harvest), adjusted for rainfall.\n- Subtropical regions typically need 6–8 irrigations across the season; tropical regions (including Karnataka) may need 20–36 irrigations due to climatic conditions, annual rainfall, planting season, crop duration, and soil type.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations:\n- Given Karnataka's high per-season irrigation requirement (32 irrigations, 266 kg water per kg sugar produced), farmers in the state should prioritize migration from furrow irrigation to drip irrigation, which the source data shows can lower net water requirement from ~1,878 mm/year to ~1,186 mm/year.\n- During monsoon disruptions, adopt the contingency schedule appropriate to the delay: for a 2-week delayed monsoon onset, continue pre-monsoon irrigation intervals (8–10 days) with alternate-furrow application and trash mulching in ratoon; for longer delays (6–8 weeks), shift to a 10-day protective irrigation interval and watch for iron deficiency, which becomes likely under prolonged dry spells (foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea is recommended in that case).\n\nICAR-IISR Current Farmer Helpline and 2024–2025 Institutional Activities — Key Facts:\n- Sugarcane seed (spring planting) online applications opened 1 February 2025 — confirms an active, current-season seed distribution channel.\n- 2024 dated activities: Parthenium Awareness Week (16–22 August 2024), sugarcane borer identification training, white grub management training, Agriculture Leadership Award 2024.\n- Farmer helpline channel: SMS/photo submission via mobile phone or email to the IISR Nodal Officer.\n\nSugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Summary:\nSugarcane is a long-duration, water-intensive crop requiring 1,500–2,500 mm of water over its growing cycle depending on climate. IISR documents phase-wise irrigation schedules, state-wise irrigation requirements, and three water-economizing techniques (skip-furrow, sprinkler, drip) that can cut water use by 30–95% while maintaining or improving productivity — highly relevant for Karnataka, which has one of the highest irrigation-water requirements among sugarcane-growing states in India.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ಸಮಯ: 2026‑07‑26 16:40 UTC (node demo‑node‑1) - ತಾಪಮಾನ: 23.0 °C (ಬದಲಾವಣೆಯಾಗಿಲ್ಲ) - ಆರ್ದ್ರತೆ: 64.2 % (ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಇಳಿದಿದೆ) - ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ: 0.0 % (ವಾಲ್ಯೂಮೆಟ್ರಿಕ್) – ಹಿಂದಿನ ಮಾಪನದಿಂದ 49 % ಕುಸಿತ (49 % → 0 %) - ಸಕ್ರಿಯ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆ: ...\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ಸಮಯ: 2026‑07‑26 16:40 UTC (node demo‑node‑1) - ತಾಪಮಾನ: 23.0 °C (ಬದಲಾವಣೆ ಇಲ್ಲ) - ಆರ್ದ್ರತೆ: 64.2 % (ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಇಳಿದಿದೆ) - ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ: 0.0 % (ವಾಲ್ಯೂಮೆಟ್ರಿಕ್) – ಹಿಂದಿನ ಮಾಪನದಿಂದ 49 % ಕುಸಿತ (49 % → 0 %) - ಸಕ್ರಿಯ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆ: **Low ...\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Vision: ಚಿತ್ರವನ್ನು ಬ್ರೌನ್ ಸ್ಪಾಟ್ (brown spot) ಎಂದು 95% ವಿಶ್ವಾಸದೊಂದಿಗೆ ವರ್ಗೀಕರಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ. - Temperature: 25.0 °C - Humidity: 55.0 % - Soil moisture: 40.0 % - Active alerts: - [warning] High humidity 88 % (ರೋಗದ ಅಪಾಯ) - ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಬ್ಬು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿದೆ, ನಾಳೆ ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ನೀರು ಹಾಕಿ.", "farmer": "ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಬ್ಬು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿದೆ, ನಾಳೆ ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ನೀರು ಹಾಕಿ."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "ನಿಮ್ಮ ಕಬ್ಬು ಚೆನ್ನಾಗಿದೆ, ನಾಳೆ ಬೆಳಿಗ್ಗೆ ನೀರು ಹಾಕಿ.", "sarvam": "Your sugarcane is good, give it water tomorrow morning.", "groq": "sugarcane irrigation timing morning water application schedule"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.81, "sarvam": 0.8273, "groq": 0.8886}, "retrieved": [{"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Main Information", "score": 0.8886, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8822, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "pests/02_iisr_current_helpline_and_2024_2025_activities.md", "section": "ICAR-IISR Current Farmer Helpline and 2024–2025 Institutional Activities — Key Facts", "score": 0.8804, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Summary", "score": 0.8736, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Active alert: **[critical] Node offline** (no contact for 50248 min).  \n- Sensor reading (2026‑09‑23 04:25 UTC):  \n  - Temperature: **27.9 °C**  \n  - Humidity: **72.4 %**  \n  - Soil moisture: **32.3 %**  \n- No prior soil‑moisture data available for trend analysis.  \n\n**Analysis**  \n- The critical node‑offline alert means the latest sensor data may be unreliable; the field’s current moisture status cannot be confirmed.  \n- With only a single soil‑moisture value (32.3 %) and no information on the crop’s growth stage (e.g., days after planting), it is not possible to determine whether the crop is in a phase that requires irrigation every 7–10 days or whether the moisture level is sufficient.  \n\n**Confidence** – **Low** (insufficient data on moisture trend and crop stage).  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n- **Immediate actions**  \n  1. Restore connectivity of the sensor node or use a backup moisture probe to obtain a current, reliable reading.  \n  2. Record the exact planting date or days‑after‑planting (DAP) for the sugarcane to identify the appropriate irrigation interval.  \n\n- **Monitoring advice**  \n  1. Once the node is online, check soil‑moisture daily for at least three consecutive days to see the trend.  \n  2. Compare the observed moisture with the irrigation schedule for the identified crop stage (e.g., ~7 days interval during grand growth, ~10 days during tillering).  \n\n- **When to seek expert help**  \n  1. If the node cannot be re‑connected within 24 hours, contact the local IISR extension officer for on‑site moisture assessment.  \n  2. If after obtaining reliable data the moisture remains around 32 % and you are unsure whether to irrigate, request a field visit to confirm crop stage and water‑requirement needs.", "claims": ["unsupport